 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1935, Australia imported 102 toads to eat beetles.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 4.278
  },
  {
   "text": "Two years later, 62,000 baby toads were hopping through the sugarcane.",
   "s": 4.578,
   "e": 8.323
  },
  {
   "text": "The plan: gobble up the cane beetles wrecking the crop.",
   "s": 8.623,
   "e": 11.024
  },
  {
   "text": "But the beetles lived high up on the cane stalks.",
   "s": 11.324,
   "e": 13.454
  },
  {
   "text": "And toads can't climb.",
   "s": 13.754,
   "e": 14.772
  },
  {
   "text": "So the toads ate everything else instead.",
   "s": 15.072,
   "e": 16.86
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse: they're poisonous. Snakes and lizards that ate them died.",
   "s": 17.16,
   "e": 20.681
  },
  {
   "text": "Today there are over 200 million. One found in 2023 weighed 2.7 kilos.",
   "s": 20.981,
   "e": 26.312
  },
  {
   "text": "Rangers named her Toadzilla.",
   "s": 26.612,
   "e": 27.781
  },
  {
   "text": "And the beetles those toads were hired to eat? Still there.",
   "s": 28.081,
   "e": 30.605
  }
 ],
